WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.960
Cloverfield is one of the most curious franchises&nbsp;
in Hollywood, with just a few seemingly disjointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.960
movies and a short manga comic to its name.&nbsp;
So what's the story behind these stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.760
and just how did the franchise come to be? This&nbsp;
is the untold truth of the Cloverfield franchise.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.280
J.J. Abrams has built his career on&nbsp;
echoing genre movies of the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.080
so it probably won't come as a surprise to&nbsp;
find that he thought up Cloverfield as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.080
way of paying tribute to classic kaiju movies.&nbsp;
However, the circumstances that informed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.080
idea were rooted in a more specific personal&nbsp;
experience — one that made Abrams realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.840
the sorry status of American-made monster&nbsp;
movies. In an interview, Abrams explained:

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.880
"I got the idea when I went to Japan [...]&nbsp;
and saw all these Godzilla toys in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.760
shops. I realised that America hasn’t&nbsp;
had a monster movie to call its own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.520
not since King Kong all those years ago anyway,&nbsp;
and thought it was time to change that."

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.080
From this toy store encounter sprang&nbsp;
a desire that fueled the creation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:14.240
not just the original Cloverfield, but an&nbsp;
entire sprawling franchise. In the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.000
Abrams did indeed create an ultra-intense monster;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.480
one that was more than capable of standing&nbsp;
up against cinema's biggest beasts.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.920
One of the most evocative aspects of&nbsp;
Cloverfield's marketing campaign was its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.440
emphasis on the beheading&nbsp;
of the Statue of Liberty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.800
The decapitated head closed out the&nbsp;
film's initial teaser trailer with a bang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.960
while a headless Lady Liberty featured as the&nbsp;
centerpiece of Cloverfield's poster. By lingering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:50.080
on this image, Cloverfield instantly crafted an&nbsp;
aura of fear and dread around its plot. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.280
the Statue of Liberty is something Americans&nbsp;
assume will always stand in New York City. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:02.160
that gets threatened by an unknown menace — well,&nbsp;
that's bound to send a chill up anyone's spine.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.400
Lady Liberty's decapitation was&nbsp;
actually inspired by the marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:13.440
materials for a classic 1980s action movie.&nbsp;
As Cloverfield director Matt Reeves told IGN:

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.280
"The inspiration came from the&nbsp;
poster of Escape from New York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.840
The poster had an image on it of the head&nbsp;
of the Statue of Liberty and that image was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.760
nowhere in the movie! And it's&nbsp;
an incredibly provocative image."

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.760
The title for the original Cloverfield didn't&nbsp;
just spring up out of thin air. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.040
the project went under several working&nbsp;
titles before settling on its final moniker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.920
During production, Cloverfield was known&nbsp;
by a number of different temporary titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.320
including "Slusho," "Chocolate&nbsp;
Outrage," and "Cheese."

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.760
Even as the movie's release date approached,&nbsp;
Cloverfield had no title to speak of, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.440
the teaser trailer only promising a release date.&nbsp;
It was later reported that the film might end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:03.520
going by the name Wreck. As it turned out, this&nbsp;
all helped to keep the first Cloverfield movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.760
cloaked under a veil of secrecy. The project's&nbsp;
marketing was defined by its cryptic nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.880
and all that constant speculation over&nbsp;
the title helped keep up that reputation.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:21.040
Cloverfield's second trailer, in November&nbsp;
2007, would eventually give the movie its name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.000
while the movie's follow-ups would further&nbsp;
turn Cloverfield into a full-blown brand.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.800
Monster movies tend to function as much as&nbsp;
sociopolitical commentary as they do opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:40.000
to watch huge beasts topple buildings. Just look&nbsp;
at the 1954 movie Godzilla, which is a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.320
reflection on Japan's nuclear anxiety. In the&nbsp;
case of Cloverfield, director Matt Reeves had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.800
very specific idea of what this particular monster&nbsp;
movie, captured through the lens of found footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.480
was meant to say about the time in which it&nbsp;
was released. Reeves told Shock Till You Drop:

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.400
"Cloverfield very much speaks to the fear and&nbsp;
anxieties of our time, how we live our lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.840
Constantly documenting things&nbsp;
and putting them up on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:14.080
sending people videos through email — we felt it&nbsp;
was very applicable to the way people feel now."

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.400
Reeves also observed how one moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:22.080
in which the lead movie's protagonists encounter&nbsp;
another person filming everything, was meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.520
remind people that there was more happening in&nbsp;
the wider world of Cloverfield. He continued:

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:33.520
"Media is so much an aspect of our lives today&nbsp;
that even evoking that idea was exciting to us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.920
That there could be different stories out there."

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.600
"You still filming?"
"Yeah. People are gonna wanna know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.840
how it all went down."

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.200
While the beast itself was absent from the&nbsp;
vast majority of the marketing for Cloverfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.520
the film itself does eventually feature&nbsp;
the creature that has come to be dubbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.360
Clover. In the film, Clover only enjoys a&nbsp;
fleeting on-screen presence, with the brevity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.720
of its appearances meant to heighten the sense of&nbsp;
fear and uncertainty felt by both the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.920
and the audience. Despite its minimal screen&nbsp;
time, though, a whole lot of care went into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.760
creature — particularly in figuring out which&nbsp;
real-world animals would come to inspire it.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.920
The primary element emphasized in the monster's&nbsp;
behavior was terror. Rather than maliciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:25.600
choosing to rampage across New York City,&nbsp;
Clover was designed to evoke wild animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.480
such as elephants or horses, that have&nbsp;
become terrified and begun to run amok.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.720
This lent an extra sense of unpredictability to&nbsp;
Clover and its actions. There was no evil plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:43.200
driving it, nor any complex motivation behind&nbsp;
all that chaos. Clover was simply engaging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.240
random fits of destruction because,&nbsp;
above all else, it was frightened.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.200
On a visual level, underwater creatures&nbsp;
such as piranhas were channeled into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.320
design of the monster's face — a nod to&nbsp;
how Clover is an extraterrestrial being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.480
that crash landed in Earth's oceans.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.480
Before he won an Oscar for Best Director for&nbsp;
his work on La La Land, and even prior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:12.240
directing Whiplash, Damien Chazelle was just&nbsp;
another artist looking for his big break.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.160
At one point, that break looked like it might&nbsp;
come from Bad Robot Productions. J.J. Abrams and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.240
company enlisted Chazelle to write and direct&nbsp;
the original version of 10 Cloverfield Lane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.040
then titled Valencia. Abrams&nbsp;
later told Empire magazine:

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.200
"Actually, [Damien Chazelle] was&nbsp;
originally coming on as a director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.240
as well, and he was working on&nbsp;
the script to direct. [...] He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.960
did incredible work with [aspects of]&nbsp;
plot and character with his draft."

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.560
The eventual director of 10 Cloverfield Lane,&nbsp;
Dan Trachtenberg, had nothing but positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.000
things to say about the feature's original&nbsp;
filmmaker. He later told The Q&amp;A Podcast:

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.520
"I have so many things to thank him for. [Because&nbsp;
he made Whiplash,] I got a chance to make a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.400
and he did a great job with the script.&nbsp;
He really gifted me with so many things."

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:09.200
When 10 Cloverfield Lane was in production,&nbsp;
its cast and crew knew it only as Valencia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:15.280
a low-budget thriller hailing from Bad Robot&nbsp;
Productions. However, as production went on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.960
something changed. Before long, Valencia had&nbsp;
become a spiritual sequel to Cloverfield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.080
and the title was altered to 10 Cloverfield Lane.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.840
"Crazy is building your ark after&nbsp;
the flood has already come!"

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.680
Rather than being the result of some brilliant&nbsp;
plan kept secret by everyone involved in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:41.280
however, attaching this project to the name&nbsp;
Cloverfield was actually a last-minute switch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.120
Speaking to MovieWeb about how this change&nbsp;
came about, director Dan Trachtenberg said:

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.480
"We wrote an original screenplay. Cloverfield&nbsp;
was not in our mind at the time. During the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:56.480
development process, the idea came up&nbsp;
that it could be in the Clover-verse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.840
We were a bit surprised initially, but now when&nbsp;
you see the movie; it makes a lot of sense in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:08.080
terms of tone, twists, turns, and the thriller&nbsp;
aspect. It was definitely the right decision."

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.880
By attaching the Cloverfield name to&nbsp;
10 Cloverfield Lane, this new thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.560
was able to stand out more easily in a crowded&nbsp;
marketplace. Moreover, following up Cloverfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.440
with an otherwise standalone project allowed&nbsp;
the film to function as a mostly separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:28.960
narrative — rather than simply supporting itself&nbsp;
on Cloverfield plot points and shout-outs.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.440
For years after the original&nbsp;
Cloverfield was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.720
news would occasionally come to light suggesting&nbsp;
that a direct follow-up was in development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:44.240
Just a few weeks after the original debuted,&nbsp;
director Matt Reeves even told Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:49.280
that they were considering a number of active&nbsp;
concepts as to where a follow-up might go.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.440
However, the busy schedules of all those&nbsp;
involved in the first Cloverfield — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.840
particularly J.J. Abrams — was constantly cited&nbsp;
as a reason why the project wasn't moving forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.800
With the release of 10 Cloverfield Lane,&nbsp;
Abrams finally had a chance to reveal why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.320
audiences hadn't seen another movie set in&nbsp;
the world of Cloverfield. He told Fandango:

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.680
"We had so much fun making&nbsp;
Cloverfield that when we were done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.600
we felt like we were done with that&nbsp;
movie. And there were a lot of ideas [for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.560
a follow-up] [...] A lot of things were thrown&nbsp;
around. But we now live in that post-Godzilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.600
post-Pacific Rim era, where those movies&nbsp;
have been made. So what would we do?"

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.640
With this in mind, Abrams and company&nbsp;
opted instead to pursue a more spiritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.480
sequel to the first movie — and&nbsp;
10 Cloverfield Lane was born.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.960
Much like 10 Cloverfield Lane, The Cloverfield&nbsp;
Paradox wasn't always meant to be a part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.760
Cloverfield franchise. Originally, it&nbsp;
was just a standalone science fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:53.600
movie called The God Particle. However,&nbsp;
according to the film's writer Oren Uziel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:10:00.480
it wasn't long before it became attached to an&nbsp;
already existing franchise. Uziel told Collider:

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.040
"[The Cloverfield Paradox] was written before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.720
10 Cloverfield Lane and the expanded Cloverfield&nbsp;
universe even existed as a thing. [...] I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:13.520
know exactly when it became a Cloverfield&nbsp;
movie. [...] I think everyone just knew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.120
if it fits — and it does — into that Cloverfield&nbsp;
world, it should, and it can only help."

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.240
Uziel emphasized that it's not easy marketing&nbsp;
an original movie of any kind these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.880
and that The God Particle's association with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.880
Cloverfield name likely gave it&nbsp;
a fighting chance at survival.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:37.360
In its long journey to release, The Cloverfield&nbsp;
Paradox went through a series of reshoots that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.040
were initially shrouded in as much secrecy as&nbsp;
anything else in the movie. However, once the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:49.120
project was officially released on Netflix, more&nbsp;
details emerged about what those reshoots had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:55.040
entailed. Apparently, they had been focused on&nbsp;
adding a new Earth-bound subplot to the movie.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.160
During a Facebook Q&amp;A session, J.J Abrams&nbsp;
noted that scenes in The Cloverfield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:05.760
Paradox following Michael Hamilton as he navigates&nbsp;
the situation on Earth were not originally part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.200
the script. They were added during these reshoots&nbsp;
at the behest of test screening audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.080
who were curious about what was happening on the&nbsp;
ground during the events of the main story, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:22.880
takes place in space. This subplot also helped set&nbsp;
up the infamous ending of The Cloverfield Paradox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.720
which explicitly connects the feature to the&nbsp;
original Cloverfield with a certain special cameo.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.442
Clover appears and roars

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:42.400
Today, A Quiet Place is its own mega-franchise,&nbsp;
one with a widely recognizable brand name that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:46.640
can generate sizable box office&nbsp;
returns with very little effort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:52.720
But once upon a time, the first Quiet Place&nbsp;
was just another original horror movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.800
Not only that, but there was once a moment&nbsp;
where it even looked like it would even be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:02.640
folded into the Cloverfield franchise.&nbsp;
Screenwriter Scott Beck told IndieWire:

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.880
"I guess it crossed our mind, and we had&nbsp;
spoken to our representatives about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:11.600
possibility. [...] We were actually talking to&nbsp;
an executive [at Paramount] about this film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:15.600
and it felt from pitch form&nbsp;
that there might be crossover."

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:19.200
Luckily, Paramount had&nbsp;
other ideas. Beck continued:

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.840
"But when we finally took the final script&nbsp;
in to Paramount, they saw it as a totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:29.280
different movie [...] I think in part because&nbsp;
conceptually it was able to stand on its own."

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.960
Given his affinity for original storytelling,&nbsp;
Beck was understandably relieved when Paramount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.880
allowed A Quiet Place to stand on its own&nbsp;
two legs. And of course, in an ironic twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:45.680
the finished movie became so popular&nbsp;
that it spawned a whole new franchise;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:51.680
one entirely distinct from the Cloverfield&nbsp;
name… and arguably more successful, too.

